    "summary": "Blues‑heavy set with standout Good Lovin', Smokestack Lightning & rare cryptic sequence",
    "blurb": "The concert delivers a blues‑laden groove highlighted by Pigpen’s powerhouse on Smokestack Lightning and Good Lovin'. A rare Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One → Cryptical sandwich adds intrigue, while Deep Elem Blues and Morning Dew give the set an acoustic edge. The second half builds strong energy with solid jamming throughout.",
    "review": "December 28, 1970 at Legion Stadium captured one of the Dead’s most blues‑infused evenings.  From the opening Smokestack Lightning through a gritty Good Lovin' that bookends the second set, the band rides a deep groove while Pigpen commands the stage with his organ and vocal snarls.  The show is punctuated by a rare Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One → Cryptical sequence that fans still talk about, and the acoustic‑folk tilt of Deep Elem Blues and Morning Dew provides nice contrast.  Multiple sources agree the performance is solid throughout, with the second set delivering especially high‑energy jams.\n\nFor listeners, the matrix recording (gd1970-12-28.mtx.tobin.87294.sbeok.flac16) offers the clearest window into this night – a crystal‑clear mix that balances Pigpen’s organ, Jerry’s Strat tone on The Other One, and the rhythm section without intrusive crowd noise.  Audience tapes also shine, but the matrix version remains the most balanced listening experience.",
